<TopicPage slug="subjects/sports-science/advanced/biomechanics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Biomechanics is <strong>the engineering ladder applied to the moving body</strong> — levers,
    forces, drag and energy return, in flesh and equipment. It makes technique teachable as
    physics rather than folklore.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="levers, force–velocity, technique physics, equipment" widget="equationStep" config={biomechCfg} />

  <h2>Measurements that changed sport</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Motion capture:</strong> markers + cameras (the film lesson\'s frame rates) give 3-D joint kinematics; force plates give ground reaction forces — together, the full mechanical story of a jump or throw. (Vicon-style systems started as clinical-gait analysis — the medicine ladder\'s biomechanics wing.)</li>
    <li><strong>ACL injuries have a mechanism:</strong> non-contact deceleration with knee valgus — biomechanics identified it, screening programmes (FIFA 11+) target it, and injury-prevention programmes cut ACL rates 30–50% where adopted (measured public health, in sport).</li>
    <li><strong>Throwing breaks the "rules":</strong> the kinetic chain (ground → hips → trunk → shoulder → arm) sequences force like a whip — the arm lags and snaps; "arm strength" is mostly a timing artefact of the chain (the pitching lesson every coach now teaches).</li>
    <li><strong>Wearables democratised the lab:</strong> GPS vests (distance, sprints, accelerations — the remote-sensing lesson\'s consumer wing) put match-load data on every professional team\'s tablet; the analytics lesson is where it lands.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Winter, Biomechanics and Motor Control of Human Movement; FIFA 11+ evaluations; the physics ladder\'s statics and fluids lessons.</p>
</TopicPage>
